From f1b3f19141f7ee5638782b46dcc54b19a43fbbaa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emre Yavuzalp <emreyavuzalp2@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 12:41:43 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 103/116] econet-eth: add PPE/FOE hardware NAT-offload engine
 (EN751221)

Add a standalone driver for the SoC's PPE (Packet Processing Engine) /
FOE (Flow Offload Engine): tuple builders, the little-endian FOE table
layout, engine bring-up/teardown, and the flow_offload/nf_flowtable
integration (econet_ppe.c/h, econet_foe.c/h, econet_ppe_offload.c). Not
wired into the driver yet; see the next commit.

Background: EN751221/EN7528 have an on-chip NAT/routing accelerator
(same "PPE" family as mtk_eth_soc's HNAT) that the stock vendor SDK
uses, but no Linux driver for it has been published. This is a
from-scratch implementation against the raw hardware, developed and
measured on TP-Link Archer C5v (EN751221): with a flow bound in
hardware, wired NAT throughput goes from ~275 Mbit/s (software
flowtable only) to ~930 Mbit/s at ~93% CPU idle.

A few hardware quirks that shaped the implementation and are easy to
get wrong if re-derived from the mtk_eth_soc HNAT driver this is
loosely modeled on:

- The FOE table is always little-endian in memory regardless of host
  endianness; __en75_ppe_foe_commit() byte-swaps each 32-bit word on
  the way in. On a big-endian host that also swaps the two u16 halves
  of any word packing two u16s (MAC/VLAN/PPPoE fields), so
  struct en75_foe_mac_info declares those pairs reversed under
  CONFIG_CPU_BIG_ENDIAN to land correctly after the swap. The eth-mangle
  rewrite path (nf_flow_table_offload's action encoding) has the same
  BE/LE split for the same reason.
- The descriptor-reported "hw_slot" for a flow is not the engine's real
  FOE table index -- it's linearly scaled (real_slot = hw_slot * 2 / 5)
  and the descriptor field is only 14 bits wide, so the scaling
  truncates for slots in the upper half of the table. Both the scale
  and the truncation have to be undone to compute the true engine slot
  in O(1) instead of falling back to a full table scan per flow.
- SEARCH_MISS is left at the hardware default (FORWARD_BUILD): the
  engine auto-learns an UNBIND entry for every miss, and the driver
  looks up that learned entry (by the corrected slot) to find where to
  place its own driver-built BIND entry, rather than racing the engine
  by search-miss-forward-and-build itself.
- The FOE cache on this SoC does not follow the mtk_eth_soc CACHE_CTL
  bitmap: enable/disable is a separate register (0x334, vendor magic
  0x33) from the clear command (0x320, write 0x4100 and poll a REQ
  bit), and clearing requires gating the cache off first. Using the
  mtk_eth_soc register layout leaves the lookup silently serving stale
  learned state.
- A bound FOE entry can only egress via GDM1's netdev; the driver
  therefore only accepts flow_offload rules whose redirect target is
  that netdev, rejecting anything (e.g. a Wi-Fi client) that would
  otherwise egress elsewhere with a hardware-forced port.

debugfs (econet_ppe/foe, foe_slot, diag, test_bind) is the inspection
interface for all of this; there is no printk on the hot path.

Signed-off-by: Emre Yavuzalp <emreyavuzalp2@gmail.com>
